<!--
  Hand-constructed, synthetic TRIAS StopEventResponse fixture - not a
  captured real response. See TriasDepartureProviderTest's class javadoc for
  the full provenance note: the publicly documented example request/response
  in https://github.com/VDVde/TRIAS/issues/1 (fetched live from GitHub while
  implementing this ticket) turned out to demonstrate a LocationInformation-
  Request/Response, not a StopEventRequest/Response as
  docs/departures-widget-plan.md's Ticket 1 section assumed - so this fixture
  is instead built by hand from the official Trias_StopEvents.xsd /
  Trias_JourneySupport.xsd / Trias_LocationSupport.xsd / Trias_Common.xsd /
  Trias_ModesSupport.xsd schema files (also fetched live from
  github.com/VDVde/TRIAS), which fully and unambiguously specify this
  element structure.

  Four StopEventResult entries, covering every case TriasDepartureProvider's
  status-derivation logic branches on:
    1. on-time, WITH real-time confirmation (EstimatedTime == TimetabledTime)
    2. on-time, WITH NO real-time data at all (no EstimatedTime element)
    3. delayed by 3 minutes (EstimatedTime 3 minutes after TimetabledTime) -
       matches the design handoff's own "+3 Min" example.
    4. cancelled (Service/Cancelled = true)

  PlannedBay/EstimatedBay also deliberately mix two real-world conventions,
  confirmed against live KVV (Karlsruhe) data: entry 1 spells the German
  word "Gleis" out in full ("Gleis 3"/mixed case "GLEIS 3") - the actual KVV
  shape, which TriasDepartureProvider.normalizePlatform() must recognize and
  reformat into the final display label "Gl. 3" (not leave in place for some
  later step to doubly relabel into "Gl. Gleis 3") - while entries 2 and 4
  keep the original bare-number convention this fixture used before that bug
  was found, so both shapes stay covered. See
  trias-stop-event-response-bus-platform.xml for the analogous "Bstg."
  (bus platform) convention, covered separately since it must NOT be
  relabelled "Gl. ".
-->
